{{nihongo|'''Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi'''|醍醐天皇|Daigo-tennō}} (4 October 1580 – 12 September 1643), born {{nihongo|'''Nabenosuke'''|鍋之助}}, known during his samurai years as {{nihongo|'''Honda Shigekatsu'''|本多重勝}}, and formally as {{nihongo|'''Prince Takahito'''|尊仁親王}}, was the 106th Emperor of Mitoyagi (modern-day [[Okaiken]]), according to the traditional order of succession. He was the eldest son of [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]] (Emperor Shirakawa) and his primary wife, [[Hatsu-no-kata]], the daughter of the renowned naval commander [[Anjō Motonobu]]. 

Daigo was the full older brother of the second Shogun, [[Honda Shigetomo]], and the older half-brother of the third Shogun, [[Honda Shigetoki]]. His reign was characterized by the strict separation of the Imperial Court from the military government (the bakufu), a system known as Dual Sovereignty. While his father and brothers waged wars to unify and control the nation, Daigo served as the spiritual and cultural anchor of the state, formally stepping into the role of Emperor in 1619, establishing the Daigo lineage, and guiding the court through the catastrophic [[Kinzō Incident]] of 1625.

==Early Life as a Samurai Heir==
Before he was aware of his imperial bloodline, Daigo was born as Nabenosuke on 4 October 1580. His birth occurred shortly after the strategic marriage between his father, the rising general Honda Shigetada, and Hatsu-no-kata in 1579. Nabenosuke spent his early childhood in the military encampments and castle towns of the Honda clan, primarily in [[Suemori]]. 

Upon his coming-of-age ceremony, he took the adult samurai name of {{nihongo|Honda Shigekatsu|本多重勝}}. As the first-born son, Shigekatsu was initially groomed to inherit the leadership of the [[Honda clan]]. He was trained in the martial arts and rudimentary statecraft alongside his younger brother, Shigetomo (Naiki). However, historical accounts note a sharp contrast between the siblings. While Shigetomo possessed a fierce, martial temperament that would later earn him the moniker of "Punisher," Shigekatsu was naturally inclined toward scholarly pursuits, poetry, and religious philosophy, often retreating to local temples to study under Jōdo-shū monks. 

During the Sengoku campaigns in [[Yagikoshu]], Shigekatsu accompanied his father and brother to the front lines. He was present during the Siege of Matsubara, although he was kept entirely out of combat, assigned instead to administrative duties and logistics within the main camp.

==The Restoration of 1601==
Shigekatsu's life was irrevocably altered following the [[Battle of Tone]] and the subsequent events of 1601. When Emperor Komei publicly identified Honda Shigetada as the lost Imperial Prince Fumihito (who had been abducted decades prior during the Yuzawa Incident), the entire Honda lineage was instantly elevated.

Shigekatsu ceased to be merely the heir of a powerful warlord; he was formally inducted into the Imperial family, erasing his Honda name from the warrior registries, and bestowed the title of {{nihongo|Prince Takahito|尊仁親王}}. He was relocated from the battlefields to the temporary imperial court in [[Okami]]. There, he was hastily educated in courtly protocols, classical literature, and imperial religious rites to prepare him for his eventual ascension, forever separating his path from his warrior brothers.

==Crown Prince and Dual Sovereignty==
In 1610, following the death of Emperor Komei, Shigetada ascended the Wisteria Throne as Emperor Shirakawa. To prevent the destabilizing concentration of both divine and military power in one individual, Shirakawa enacted the Edict of the Two Realms ({{nihongo|Ryōkai-no-Rei|両界の令}}). 

Under this system, the state was split. Shigetada relinquished the title of Shogun to his second son, Shigetomo, giving him absolute control over the military, land distribution, and infrastructure. Meanwhile, Prince Takahito was officially designated as the Crown Prince and Imperial successor, tasked exclusively with managing the court nobles (kuge), foreign diplomacy, and the spiritual well-being of the nation. 

It was during this period as Crown Prince that Takahito married {{nihongo|[[Taisei-in]]|泰清院}}, a noblewoman of deep intellect and influence who provided vital support in navigating the complex, often treacherous politics of the court nobles. Together, they fostered an environment suited to Takahito's scholarly nature, heavily patronizing the arts and traditional Sanese theater.

==Ascension to the Throne==
In 1619, at the age of 63, Emperor Shirakawa formally abdicated to establish a "Cloistered Rule" (Insei) from [[Toyozawa Castle]]. Prince Takahito ascended the throne, taking the name Emperor Daigo. By choosing this name, he formally established the Daigo imperial lineage, signaling a new era of cultural renaissance distinct from the warring eras of his predecessors.

Despite being the reigning Emperor, Daigo held very little practical political power during the early years of his reign. His father, the Retired Emperor, continued to manipulate the grand strategy of the realm from Toyozawa, while Shogun Shigetomo enforced the laws from Okami. Daigo willingly accepted this dynamic, focusing his efforts on rebuilding the cultural heritage of Mitoyagi that had been devastated by decades of civil war. He commissioned the restoration of numerous ancient shrines, standardized the state's official Jōdo-shū Buddhist practices, and fostered a cultural renaissance within the capital alongside Empress Taisei-in.

==The Kinzō Crisis of 1625==
{{Main|Kinzō Incident}}
The fragile balance of the Dual Sovereignty system shattered in the summer of 1625. The aggressive Kinzō Reforms, authored by the Retired Emperor and enforced by Shogun Shigetomo, triggered a rebellion among the conservative fudai daimyos.

On 6 June 1625, the Ii clan, led by [[Ii Naotaka]], launched a surprise attack on Toyozawa Castle, resulting in the assassination of Retired Emperor Shirakawa. Twelve days later, Shogun Shigetomo was mortally wounded while leading a reckless, grief-fueled siege against the Ii stronghold at [[Ichijoudani]]. 

Emperor Daigo, isolated in the Okami Imperial Palace, suddenly found himself as the senior surviving member of his immediate family. The realm was on the brink of total collapse, with the military exhausted and regional lords preparing to resume the Sengoku wars. 

In this critical moment, Emperor Daigo utilized his supreme spiritual authority to stabilize the government. He immediately summoned his younger half-brother, [[Honda Shigetoki]], a calm and competent bureaucrat, and formally invested him as the third Shogun on 10 July 1625. Together, the two surviving brothers made the difficult, deeply unpopular decision to forgo vengeance. Daigo gave his imperial blessing to Shigetoki's Edict of Clemency, famously known as the Great Reconciliation, which pardoned the Ii clan and prevented a nationwide civil war. Daigo's public declaration—that the cycle of bloodshed had taken enough of his family and must end—was instrumental in forcing the furious Honda loyalists to stand down.

==Later Reign and Legacy==
Following the Kinzō Incident, Emperor Daigo and Shogun Shigetoki forged a highly effective, cooperative relationship that defined the next two decades. With Shigetoki managing the stabilization of the bakufu and slowing the controversial land reforms, Daigo retreated into a period of profound mourning and spiritual reflection.

He dedicated the remainder of his reign to pacifism and cultural preservation. He personally oversaw the enshrinement of his father and brother, granting Shigetomo the posthumous title of "God of Seals," which facilitated the modern festival traditions at Ichijoudani. Daigo was also instrumental in the formal compilation of the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'', ensuring the history of his family's rise and the tragedies of 1625 were recorded for posterity.

Emperor Daigo passed away peacefully on 12 September 1643 at the age of 62. Because his reign successfully entrenched the separation of the ceremonial throne from the military shogunate, historians credit him with establishing the stable, constitutional template that allowed the Imperial House to survive the entirety of the Honda Period intact. The Daigo lineage he established would carry the Chrysanthemum Throne into the modern era.

==In Popular Culture==
Because his life was largely defined by his pacifism and ceremonial duties, Emperor Daigo is often portrayed as a tragic, gentle figure in Okaiken historical dramas, serving as the emotional counterweight to his violent father and brother. In the record-breaking 1983 Taisen Drama ''[[Honda Shigetomo (Taisen drama)|Honda Shigetomo]]'', he was depicted as the sorrowful observer of his family's self-destruction. He features more prominently in the 2008 series ''Genna Revolt'', where his partnership with the third Shogun, Shigetoki, is explored in depth during the aftermath of the Kinzō Incident.

